{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310624,"nonce":"IianDjAZWsl1uzqq/dvTUAIjQ2Q=","title":"Four interlocking bugs that all surfaced loading Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M end-to-end. FIXED. (1) GGUF dtype 14 is Q6_K (210 bytes/block), not ...","message":"All four are fixed (workarounds for (3) and (4); proper fixes for (1) and (2)). llama-chat on Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M:\n- Used 2.45 GB resident (was OOM-killed at \u003e40 GB)\n- Produces real vocabulary tokens (was `!!!!!`)\n- Output: `\"The capital of France is|� ofTags� }�� balance\"` — pipeline-correct, sampling still off\n- 128/128 haxe regression + 709 lib tests + tiny-transformer all green\n\n\nQ6_K layout (210 bytes per 256-element super-block):\n- `ql[128]`: lower 4 bits of each 6-bit quant\n- `qh[64]`:  upper 2 bits of each 6-bit quant (4 quants packed per byte)\n- `scales[16]`: i8 per-sub-block scales (16 sub-blocks × 16 weights)\n- `d`: f16 super-block scale\n- Final: `y = d * scale * (q - 32)` where q is the recombined 6-bit value\n\nFixes landed:\n- `runtime/src/quant.rs`: added `QSCHEME_Q6_K`, `Q6_K_BLOCK_BYTES = 210`, `dequant_q6_k_block`, `rayzor_qtensor_from_bytes_q6_k`, extended `rayzor_qtensor_dequant` and `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32` to dispatch on scheme.\n- `compiler/src/stdlib/qtensor.rs`: extern declaration + `build_qtensor_from_bytes_q6_k` MIR wrapper.\n- `compiler/haxe-std/rayzor/ds/QTensor.hx`: `fromBytesQ6K` API.\n- `compiler/src/stdlib/runtime_mapping.rs`: `QTensor.fromBytesQ6K` mapping.\n- `runtime/src/plugin_impl.rs`: symbol export.\n- `nue/nue/loader/GGUFReader.hx`: `tensorByteSize` case 14 → `blockedSize(nElem, 256, 210)` (was 144); case 12 (Q4_K) → `blockedSize(nElem, 256, 144)` (was the also-correct-by-coincidence `(32, 18)`).\n- `nue/nue/loader/GGUFLoader.hx`: separate `case 12` (Q4_K via `decodeQ4KMRaw`) and `case 14` (Q6_K via new `decodeQ6KRaw`).\n\nUnit test: `q6_k_block_dequant_matches_reference` verifies against the llama.cpp formula. Python reference dequant of Llama 3.2 1B's row 0 / 791 matches our runtime output exactly.\n\n\n`runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs:3279` returned `-\u003e f32`, but Haxe's `Bytes.getFloat: Float` is f64. On x86-64 sys-v ABI: caller reads xmm0 as f64; the f32 bits sit in the low 32 with garbage high 32. F32 `2.45` became F64 `2.1e-310`.\n\nSymptom: every F32 GGUF tensor (norm gains, RoPE freqs) loaded as zeros after `Tensor.fromArray(floats, DType.F32).reshape(...)` — because subnormals flushed to zero. RMSNorm gain = 0 → `n.mul(weight) = 0` → transformer block acted as identity → all logits zero → argmax = 0 every step (token 0 = `!`).\n\nFix: change return type to `f64`, promote `as f64` before returning. Also update `runtime_mapping.rs` `getFloat` mappings (3 entries: `rayzor_Bytes`, `haxe_io_Bytes`, `Bytes`) from `IrTypeDescriptor::F32` → `F64`.\n\n\n`compiler/src/stdlib/array.rs:362` — `build_array_push` MIR wrapper takes `value: Any` and calls `haxe_array_push_i64(arr, value)`. So `arr.push(v:Float)` passes the f64 through an i64-typed parameter. On x86-64 sys-v, f64 goes in xmm0 / i64 in rdi — the i64 callee reads rdi which has stale/zero bits.\n\nEven with (2) fixed (`getFloat` returns correct f64 = 2.45), `floats.push(getFloat(pos))` loses the value: `direct = raw.getFloat(0)` = 2.45 ✓ but `floats[0]` = garbage.\n\nWorkaround landed: added `rayzor_tensor_from_bytes_f32` that memcpys raw f32 bytes directly into a fresh F32 tensor, bypassing the `Array\u003cFloat\u003e` round-trip. Exposed as `Tensor.fromBytesF32(bytes, shape)`. GGUFLoader's F32 path now uses it.\n\n**Compiler fix still pending**: `build_array_push` should dispatch to `haxe_array_push_f64` (or do an i64-bitcast on the f64 value, preserving bits) when the element type is Float. Until that lands, any user code doing `arr.push(someF64)` cross-file will silently lose precision. Same shape as `bugs_null_int_cross_file_assign` — a type-aware coercion gap at the calling-convention boundary.\n\n\nSee [[bugs-null-int-cross-file-assign]] — documented separately. Workaround in `nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx`: `var unboxed = ctxRaw + 0;` (arithmetic forces unbox).\n\n\nAfter (1)-(4) llama-chat runs the forward+sample loop end-to-end and produces real vocabulary tokens. Output is not coherent yet (`\"The capital of France is|� ofTags� }�� balance\"`). Candidates for next investigation:\n\n1. **RoPE position**: prefill uses `positionOffset = cache.currentLen` (0 at start), decode step adds 1. Verify rotations match HF/llama.cpp for the same token sequence.\n2. **GQA attention numerics**: scaling, mask, softmax — if any is off, sampling picks plausible-but-wrong tokens.\n3. **Sampler**: `ArgmaxSampler` picks the literal max. Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct expects temperature + top-p — for a deterministic check, argmax should still produce reasonable text on simple completion prompts.\n4. **BPE decode**: weird unicode in output may be byte-fallback tokens being decoded raw bytes; check the tokenizer's special-token / byte-fallback handling.\n\nSuggested smoke test: prompt `\"The capital of France is\"` should produce at least one of `\" Paris\"` or `\" Paris,\"` in the first few tokens.\n\n\n- [[bugs-null-int-cross-file-assign]] — same shape (type-aware coercion missing at call boundary), for Int.\n- [[bugs-dtype-enum-cross-file-pointer]] — same shape, for enum variants.\n- [[bugs-q4-km-layout-and-linear-convention]] — earlier Q4 layout work, still correct.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_gguf_q6k_and_f32_load` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}